      "In 1865, the United States emerged from a civil war that had killed roughly two percent of its population. The task before the nation was unprecedented: reintegrate eleven defeated states and fold four million formerly enslaved people into political life. The period that followed — Reconstruction — remains one of the most ambitious experiments in democracy anywhere in the world, and one of its most instructive failures.",
      "The constitutional amendments passed during this era permanently redefined citizenship. The Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery. The Fourteenth, ratified in 1868, guaranteed due process and equal protection to all persons born on American soil. The Fifteenth prohibited denying the vote on the basis of race. On paper, these provisions constituted a second founding. In practice, their enforcement depended entirely on federal resolve — and that resolve was finite.",
      "For about a decade, Black Americans voted in extraordinary numbers. Hundreds held state legislatures seats; sixteen served in Congress. Public education systems were founded across the South for students of every race. The Freedmen's Bureau negotiated labor contracts, provided medical care, and argued — sometimes successfully — that freedom required more than the absence of chains.",
      "Yet the counterrevolution was organized and patient. Paramilitary groups intimidated voters. Southern legislatures crafted poll taxes, literacy tests, and grandfather clauses that technically applied to everyone while reliably excluding Black citizens. By 1877, federal troops had withdrawn, and what historians call Redemption — the restoration of white supremacist rule — was complete across most of the former Confederacy.",
      "The Supreme Court accelerated the retreat. In the Civil Rights Cases of 1883, it struck down public-accommodation protections. In Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), it blessed segregation so long as facilities were 'separate but equal' — a standard no state ever seriously met. For the next six decades, Jim Crow operated not as a deviation from American law but, in large measure, as its local expression.",
      "Why does this history matter for understanding modern America? Because the institutions built to circumnavigate Reconstruction — poll taxes became literacy tests became opaque registration rules — reveal how democracies fail: rarely by announcing failure, usually by redefining eligibility. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 finally armed the federal government against such devices, and Black voter registration surged within years.",
      "Historians remain divided on counterfactuals. Was Reconstruction doomed by economic exhaustion and Northern indifference? Or did it fail because it ended too soon — a revolution granted a decade rather than a generation? Either way, its afterlife is measurable. School funding formulas, felony disenfranchisement, and the geographic distribution of poverty all trace lineages back to decisions made between 1865 and 1877.",
      "Eric Foner called Reconstruction 'America's unfinished revolution,' a phrase that captures both its radical promise and its incomplete delivery. Nations, like individuals, tend to repeat what they decline to remember. The amendments survived; the society they imagined is still being negotiated.",
