      "Every general-purpose technology arrives wrapped in two contradictory stories. One predicts abundance: machines absorb drudgery and humans ascend to more interesting work. The other predicts obsolescence: machines absorb tasks, and the humans attached to those tasks become redundant. Artificial intelligence has inherited both narratives simultaneously — which is precisely why sober analysis is difficult and necessary.",
      "The economic literature offers a useful tripartite distinction. Automation substitutes for labor: a system performs a task that previously required a person. Augmentation complements labor: a system makes a person measurably better at the same task. And new-task creation generates work that simply did not exist before — the category economist David Autor credits for most historical employment resilience. Radiology illustrates the tension. Machine-learning systems now detect certain tumors with radiologist-level accuracy, yet employment of radiologists has grown, because demand for imaging expanded once imaging became cheaper and faster.",
      "Large language models complicate this tidy taxonomy, because they attack cognitive breadth rather than narrow procedure. Earlier automation targeted routine manual and clerical work — payroll processing, assembly lines. Modern systems draft contracts, summarize research, generate working code, and conduct passable customer conversations. The exposed territory is no longer defined by repetitiveness but by predictability: if the input, the reasoning steps, and the evaluation criteria can be fully specified, a capable model can approximate the output.",
      "Wage effects may arrive before employment effects. When AI raises the productivity of senior professionals, junior professionals lose the routine assignments through which they once learned. Entry-level hiring in affected fields thins first; the career ladder loses a rung rather than the whole ladder collapsing. Firms that quietly slow graduate recruitment today are making an inference about tomorrow's org chart.",
      "Institutional adaptation determines whether disruption becomes displacement. electrification took four decades partly because factories had to be physically rebuilt around electric motors; the payoff appeared only after organizations redesigned themselves. Software diffused far faster. AI diffusion is faster still, because the 'installation' is often a subscription. But organizational redesign — redefining roles, evaluation metrics, and training pipelines — cannot be purchased by subscription, and history suggests it is the binding constraint.",
      "Policy responses divide into three families. Retraining programs have a mediocre record at scale, though well-designed sectoral partnerships outperform generic courses. Safety nets decoupled from specific jobs — portable benefits, wage insurance — address churn without freezing structure. And redistribution through taxation of capital gains versus labor raises old political questions with new urgency. None of these choices is technically impossible; all are politically contested.",
      "A final complication deserves emphasis: measurement. Productivity statistics struggled to capture the value of free goods like search and email; they struggle equally with AI copilots whose benefit shows up as quality rather than volume. If we cannot see the gains clearly, we will argue past each other about whether they exist.",
